messages:
olivia_support: Hey team — customer hit a 400 when calling our tool endpoint. They sent 'annotate_file' via function calling and response was \"Schema validation error: parameter 'metadata' expected object but got string\". Attaching the request they used below.

olivia_support: ```
POST /v1/call_tool
Content-Type: application/json
{
  \"tool\": \"annotate_file\",
  \"args\": {
    \"file_url\": \"s3://acme-bucket/report.pdf\",
    \"metadata\": \"{\\\"project\\\":\\\"quarterly-report\\\",\\\"owner\\\":\\\"jane.doe@acme.com\\\"}\"
  }
}
```

olivia_support: customer says they constructed metadata in their app and did JSON.stringify(metadata) before sending. They also pasted the raw body above.

customer_jane: confirming — our frontend was doing JSON.stringify on the metadata object and then embedding it into the args object (we thought the SDK would handle it). Got the 400 almost immediately.

dan_engineer: thanks. This looks like a classic double-encoding issue. The tool schema expects metadata to be an object (map), but the client sent a string containing JSON. Our validator sees a string and fails type-check.

kai_sre: do we get any parser confusion from the escaped quotes inside that string? Should be fine since it's valid JSON overall; the inner JSON is just a string value. The validation layer rejects it because of type mismatch, not a parse error.

olivia_support: error payload from our API: ```
{
  \"error\": {
    \"type\": \"invalid_request_error\",
    \"message\": \"Schema validation failed: args.metadata (expected object)\"
  }
}
```

dan_engineer: repro steps for us: try sending the same body but with metadata encoded as a JSON string vs as an object. I can repro locally. Example that fails (customer):
```
{
  \"tool\": \"annotate_file\",
  \"args\": {
    \"file_url\": \"s3://acme-bucket/report.pdf\",
    \"metadata\": \"{\\\"project\\\":\\\"quarterly-report\\\",\\\"owner\\\":\\\"jane.doe@acme.com\\\"}\"
  }
}
```
Example that succeeds:
```
{
  \"tool\": \"annotate_file\",
  \"args\": {
    \"file_url\": \"s3://acme-bucket/report.pdf\",
    \"metadata\": { \"project\": \"quarterly-report\", \"owner\": \"jane.doe@acme.com\" }
  }
}
```

dan_engineer: root cause: client-side JSON.stringify called on nested object before handing to SDK. SDK then marshals the overall args object into JSON and we receive a string in metadata.

olivia_support: customer — can you try sending metadata as an object (no extra stringify) or pass metadata as a JS object into our SDK call? They said they're using the raw HTTP call via fetch so I gave them the working example above.

customer_jane: tried the object form, it worked. Thanks. For our issue we saw this pattern across a few quickstart wrappers — might be worth adding a note in the troubleshooting doc.

kai_sre: Agreed. Suggestion: add a short bullet in support KB explaining \"do not stringify nested objects; pass native objects as args\" and a snippet for common clients (fetch, curl, python requests) showing correct vs incorrect. Also consider improving the error message to say \"expected object but got string — did you JSON.stringify a nested object?\" to speed customer triage.

dan_engineer: I can file a small change request to tweak the validator error text and add an internal repro test. Also adding a unit test to ensure the tooling returns a clearer hint.

olivia_support: marking this resolved for the customer. Will add KB link to the case. Thanks all. :tada:

repro-bot: automated note — reproduced failure in staging with customer's payload at 2026-03-15T14:22:30Z. Created issue INF-2048.
